\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The model at the level of the summary: one box per part.\relax }}{3}{figure.caption.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Every unit and connection of the explained model, nested by part. Blue: positive weight (raises the score); red: negative weight (lowers it); thicker: larger weight. Orange outline and up arrow: raises the final prediction; teal and down arrow: lowers it; grey and double arrow: depends.\relax }}{5}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The whole model: what feeds it, one level down.\relax }}{6}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The whole model: its units and every connection into them. Blue: positive weight (raises the score); red: negative weight (lowers it); thicker: larger weight. Orange outline and up arrow: raises the final prediction; teal and down arrow: lowers it; grey and double arrow: depends.\relax }}{6}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The whole model: Each input's change in the predicted value for the typical case across the whole model, largest first (orange: raises it, teal: lowers it).\relax }}{7}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Heart rate gate: what feeds it, one level down.\relax }}{7}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Heart rate gate: its units and every connection into them. Blue: positive weight (raises the score); red: negative weight (lowers it); thicker: larger weight. Orange outline and up arrow: raises the final prediction; teal and down arrow: lowers it; grey and double arrow: depends.\relax }}{8}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Heart rate gate: Response curves through this part: the typical case (bold), the 10th to 90th percentile of individual patients (band), the fold or step point (dashed) with its spread across them (grey), and the observed values (ticks).\relax }}{8}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Heart rate gate: How the effect differs between groups: one curve per group, for the typical case of each group.\relax }}{9}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Heart rate gate: The predicted value for the typical case at each level, through this part (n = training patients; grey: fewer than 20).\relax }}{9}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Heart rate gate: This part's own output, unit h4 (the output of 'Heart rate gate'), on its own scale (its activation), with every other input at the typical case; it is active for 104 of 242. Bold: the typical case; band: 10th to 90th percentile across individuals; dashed: where it switches on; ticks: the data.\relax }}{9}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Heart rate gate: This part's hidden units: each unit's output against its main numeric input (top) and how often it is active (bottom).\relax }}{10}{figure.caption.23}%
